\begin{abstract}
Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models report high Collision Avoidance Rate (CAR) on safety benchmarks, but this reported safety can be illusory: an artifact of action collapse rather than learned avoidance. On SafeLIBERO, an SDF-augmented Pi0 configuration achieves 91.5\% CAR with 0\% Task Success Rate (TSR); 99.6\% of its safe episodes exhibit displacement below 0.1\,mm, and 73.9\% remain below 1\,$\mu$m. The robot is frozen, not safe. In contrast, Pi0.5 achieves 74.7\%\,$\pm$\,1.5\% TSR and 26.8\%\,$\pm$\,1.6\% CAR with zero fake safe episodes, all exceeding 288\,mm displacement. We propose displacement-filtered CAR (CAR$_\text{f}$), which excludes episodes below a displacement threshold $\tau_d$. The bimodal displacement distribution (five orders of magnitude gap between collapsed and active episodes) makes $\tau_d$ robust across $[2, 200]$\,mm; applying CAR$_\text{f}$ collapses Pi0+A1 from 91.5\% to approximately 0\%. Beyond metric contamination, EE-only Signed Distance Field (SDF) refinement is structurally counterproductive: three ground-truth oracle variants achieve 73.1--76.9\% CAR, all below unrefined baselines (82--83\%), because SDF gradients that repel the EE simultaneously drive arm links into obstacle volumes. Attribution experiments show that collision-free demonstrations are sufficient for collision avoidance learning: LoRA-only fine-tuning raises CAR by ${\sim}$28 points (26.8\% $\to$ 54.5\%) and matches LoRA+SDF at $\lambda \in \{0.005, 0.01, 0.1\}$ ($p > 0.1$), rendering EE-only SDF supervision redundant across a 20$\times$ loss weight range. Fine-tuning produces genuine avoidance with zero frozen episodes but incurs moderate TSR degradation of ${\sim}$21--23 percentage points (74.7\% $\to$ 51.5--54.0\%). Displacement filtering is necessary for meaningful VLA safety evaluation. EE-only SDF supervision is neither necessary nor structurally sufficient for collision avoidance: across a 20$\times$ range of loss weights ($\lambda \in [0.005, 0.1]$), collision-free demonstrations account for the entire avoidance gain with identical per-task patterns, while ground-truth oracle refinement produces more collisions by redistributing them to unmonitored arm links.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}\label{sec:intro}

Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models must avoid collisions to operate safely in physical environments~\cite{li2026vlasafety}. The SafeLIBERO benchmark~\cite{hu2025vlsa} provides the first systematic protocol for measuring collision avoidance in VLA manipulation, and recent methods, including SDF-augmented training~\cite{zhang2025safevla}, constraint-based planning~\cite{yang2025cape}, and collision-free demonstrations~\cite{cofreeevla2026}, report high Collision Avoidance Rate (CAR). Our SDF-augmented Pi0~\cite{black2024pi0} reaches 91.5\% CAR across 800 episodes. The problem appears close to solved.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{fig1_teaser}
\caption{The fake safety problem in VLA collision avoidance evaluation. Standard CAR classifies a frozen robot as safe because it produces zero collisions (left). CAR$_\text{f}$ filters out action-collapsed episodes where displacement falls below $\tau_d$, revealing that Pi0+A1's 91.5\% safety score drops to ${\approx}$\,0\% (center). Pi0.5 exhibits genuine avoidance with active motion above 288\,mm in all safe episodes, and CAR$_\text{f}$ equals raw CAR (right).}
\label{fig:teaser}
\end{figure}

It is not (Figure~\ref{fig:teaser}). Of the 732 collision-free episodes, 99.6\% exhibit displacement below 0.1\,mm; TSR is 0\% across all 800 episodes. The robot is frozen, not safe. The same pattern holds across all Pi0 configurations (Section~\ref{sec:fake_safety}). We term this \emph{fake safety}: \emph{action collapse} (near-zero actions producing zero collisions) renders reported CAR illusory, and standard evaluation cannot distinguish inaction from deliberate avoidance~\cite{karwowski2024goodhart}.

We present a systematic investigation of this problem across the Pi0 and Pi0.5~\cite{black2024pi0} model families on SafeLIBERO, spanning 70+ experiments and 6000+ evaluation episodes. Three findings emerge.

\textbf{Fake safety is pervasive.} We introduce displacement-filtered CAR (CAR$_\text{f}$), which excludes episodes below a displacement threshold $\tau_d$. CAR$_\text{f}$ collapses Pi0+A1 from 91.5\% to ${\approx}$\,0\% (3/732 safe episodes survive). The bimodal displacement distribution makes $\tau_d$ robust across five orders of magnitude. Pi0.5 validates the metric: TSR\,=\,74.7\%, CAR\,=\,26.8\%, with every safe episode above 288\,mm displacement and CAR$_\text{f}$ equal to raw CAR (Section~\ref{sec:fake_safety}).

\textbf{EE-only SDF refinement is structurally counterproductive.} Three ground-truth oracle variants all achieve \emph{lower} CAR than unrefined baselines (73.1--76.9\% vs.\ 82--83\%). The SDF gradient repels the EE but rotates arm links into obstacle volumes (Section~\ref{sec:ee_only}).

\textbf{Collision-free demonstrations are sufficient for collision avoidance.} LoRA fine-tuning on Pi0.5 raises CAR by ${\sim}$28 points (26.8\% $\to$ 54.5\%) with zero frozen episodes; adding SDF supervision across a 20$\times$ range of loss weights ($\lambda \in \{0.005, 0.01, 0.1\}$) produces no measurable benefit; the per-task avoidance pattern is identical with and without geometric supervision, indicating that the collision-free demonstration distribution, not EE-only SDF supervision, accounts for all observed avoidance learning on this benchmark (Section~\ref{sec:avoidance_source}).

Our contributions are:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{CAR$_\text{f}$ metric:} displacement-filtered CAR that separates genuine avoidance from action collapse, robust across $\tau_d \in [2, 200]$\,mm.
    \item \textbf{Fake safety diagnosis:} 70+ experiments across Pi0/Pi0.5 showing $>$99\% of Pi0 safe episodes are action-collapse artifacts.
    \item \textbf{EE-only SDF is counterproductive:} ground-truth oracle refinement \emph{reduces} CAR below unrefined baselines by redistributing collisions to unmonitored arm links.
    \item \textbf{Demonstration sufficiency:} on SafeLIBERO, collision-free demonstrations account for all measured CAR gains (${\sim}$28\,pp); EE-only SDF at $\lambda \in [0.005, 0.1]$ (20$\times$ range) adds nothing beyond the demonstration signal.
\end{itemize}

